    "rationale": "No public polling is available for this race, so the forecast is based solely on structural factors and carries low confidence. Incumbent Mark Alford benefits from serving on the House Appropriations Committee, high name recognition from 23 years as a Kansas City TV news anchor, and the historical Republican lean of the 4th District. However, a newly redrawn congressional map (signed September 2025) added part of Kansas City, shifting the district's partisan composition in ways that are not yet well-understood. The map is controversial and faces legal challenges and a citizen-led referendum effort, injecting further uncertainty. On the Democratic side, a competitive seven-candidate primary (August 4, 2026) features several credible contenders — including Jordan Herrera (endorsed by the Missouri AFL-CIO) and Jeanette Cass (who previously ran for the seat) — but no consensus nominee has emerged yet, which modestly aids the incumbent in the near term. The Libertarian candidate, Thomas Holbrook, will also appear on the general ballot. On balance, Republican advantages in incumbency and district history suggest a meaningful but not commanding edge, tempered by redistricting uncertainty and the complete absence of polling data.",
